<div class="ephemera-page">

  <section class="ep-hero">
    <div class="ep-kicker">Provenance · Documentation · Access</div>
    <h2>Every item has a story</h2>
    <p>Preserve information about where materials came from, who contributed them, and whether any preferences or restrictions accompany a donation.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>Why donor information matters</h2>
    <p>Information about the people and organizations who contribute materials provides useful context for the collection. It can help staff understand the relationships among creators, publishers, bookstores, events, and comics communities.</p>
    <p>It also supports appropriate acknowledgment, responsible handling, and future decisions about display and access.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>Two kinds of information to preserve</h2>
    <div class="ep-grid">
      <article class="ep-card">
        <h3>Item-level provenance</h3>
        <p>Information that belongs in the inventory, including the donor or source and the date received.</p>
        <p>This helps staff understand the item's history and identify its source.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ep-card">
        <h3>Donation-level documentation</h3>
        <p>Correspondence, donor information, permission statements, restrictions, and any related paperwork.</p>
        <p>Retain these materials separately from the item-level spreadsheet record as appropriate.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>At the time of receipt</h2>

    <article class="ep-step">
      <div class="ep-number">01</div>
      <h3>Identify the source</h3>
      <p>Record the name of the person, publisher, bookstore, organization, or other source that provided the materials, when known.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="ep-step">
      <div class="ep-number">02</div>
      <h3>Record the date received</h3>
      <p>Use the actual receipt date when available. Do not substitute an item's publication date or an estimated date of creation.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="ep-step">
      <div class="ep-number">03</div>
      <h3>Retain accompanying information</h3>
      <p>Keep relevant emails, letters, donor notes, acknowledgments, and other supporting documentation together in the appropriate location.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="ep-step">
      <div class="ep-number">04</div>
      <h3>Identify preferences or restrictions</h3>
      <p>Pay attention to requests about attribution, display, reproduction, online use, privacy, or other conditions.</p>
    </article>

    <article class="ep-step">
      <div class="ep-number">05</div>
      <h3>Refer questions to the librarian</h3>
      <p>Do not independently promise special treatment, public acknowledgment, reproduction rights, or restrictions on behalf of the library.</p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>Official gifts versus informal contributions</h2>
    <div class="ep-panel">
      <p>Not every contribution of ephemera follows the same administrative process as a formal book or resource donation. The appropriate documentation and acknowledgment depend on the nature of the contribution and the applicable college procedures.</p>
      <p>Staff should not independently determine the official gift status of materials or make commitments about tax documentation, valuation, or Foundation acknowledgment.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="ep-warning">
      <strong>When in doubt:</strong> Preserve the materials and correspondence, record the available source information, and ask the librarian how the contribution should be handled.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>Restrictions and preferences to watch for</h2>
    <div class="ep-grid">
      <article class="ep-card">
        <h3>Attribution</h3>
        <p>Does the contributor want their name, business, or organization credited?</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ep-card">
        <h3>Display preferences</h3>
        <p>Are there requests about how, when, or whether materials should be displayed?</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ep-card">
        <h3>Reproduction and online use</h3>
        <p>Are there limitations or questions about scanning, photographing, or publishing images of materials?</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ep-card">
        <h3>Privacy or access</h3>
        <p>Does the contribution contain personal information, sensitive content, or an expressed access restriction?</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>What should staff do with restrictions?</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Preserve the original communication or documentation.</li>
      <li>Do not discard or alter the materials.</li>
      <li>Bring the restriction or request to the librarian's attention.</li>
      <li>Follow the librarian's direction before processing, displaying, reproducing, or providing access to affected materials.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Do not assume that a donation automatically grants permission to reproduce or publish an image of an item.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>Recording donor information in the inventory</h2>
    <p>Use the <strong>Donor + date received</strong> field to record the source and receipt date for each item.</p>
    <p>When multiple items come from the same contribution, record the relevant donor information for each item so that individual inventory records remain meaningful.</p>
    <p>Do not put confidential correspondence or detailed donor notes in the general inventory spreadsheet.</p>
  </section>

  <div class="ep-footer">Clark College Libraries · Comics &amp; Graphic Novels Ephemera · Provenance reference</div>
</div>
